# Test: a piped line a per-command policy denies is refused, and the policy sees the chain
#
# The one question: under T-6 A, does the TACACS+ authorizer receive the typed
# line with its chain as tokens after the arguments, so a per-command policy
# that denies a chain refuses it with the permission-denied answer and no
# handler runs, while the same command without that chain is allowed? The
# authorizer wrappers' unit tests prove the tokens pass through; only a daemon
# proves the SSH route hands the authorizer the typed line instead of the
# folded one, against a real TACACS+ server.
#
# VALIDATES: spec-pipe-chain-honored-on-every-command-surface AC-27 (SSH) and
#   the security negative of the Goal Validation table. User story 10.
# PREVENTS: the authorizer judging a line nobody typed: today SSH folds
#   `show bgp rib | count` into `show bgp rib count` before isAuthorized, so a
#   policy written against what operators type never sees the chain.
#
# Red today: the authorizer receives `show bgp rib count`, which holds no
#   "| count", so the mock passes it and the count is printed.
#
# Owed before promotion: the REST and gRPC rows of AC-27 and the read-only
# caller of AC-28 (`request ... | count` over REST execute and REST stream),
# with the read-only credential configured as test/plugin/tacacs-readonly.ci
# does; package 3b adds those lines with isAuthorized.
#
# Method: test/plugin/tacacs-author.ci's AAA config, with the standalone
# `le mock tacacs` server on $PORT (internal/test/mock/tacacs: --author-deny
# refuses an AUTHOR request whose command holds the substring) and `ze cli -c`
# over SSH on $PORT2, as test/ui/cli-argument-refused-plugin-route.ci. The
# standalone mock writes its own stderr, one record per line
# (internal/test/mock/tacacs records.go Record.Line), with the command in
# plain double quotes. test/plugin/tacacs-author.ci matches `\\"` because its
# mock runs inside the daemon, whose log line escapes them; this one does not.
#
# The permission-denied answer is plugin.UnauthorizedMessage, "command
# restricted by access control" (internal/component/plugin/types.go), which
# the SSH route prints as `error: command restricted by access control`.

option=timeout:value=60s
option=env:var=ZE_CONFIG_DIR:value=.
option=env:var=XDG_DATA_HOME:value=.
option=env:var=ze.ssh.password:value=testpass

stdin=init-answers:terminator=EOF_INIT
admin
testpass
127.0.0.1
$PORT2
EOF_INIT

tmpfs=aaa.conf:terminator=EOF_CONF
system {
	authentication {
		tacacs {
			server 127.0.0.1 { port $PORT; key "ze-mock-key"; }
			timeout 2
			authorization true
			accounting true
		}
		tacacs-profile 15 { profile [ admin ]; }
	}
	authorization {
		profile admin { run { default-action allow; } edit { default-action allow; } }
	}
}
environment {
	ssh {
		enabled true
		server main {
			ip 127.0.0.1;
			port $PORT2;
		}
	}
}
bus {
	endpoint bgp-rib {
		internal bgp-rib;
	}
}
EOF_CONF

cmd=background:seq=1:exec=le mock tacacs --port $PORT --key ze-mock-key --user admin:testpass:15 --author-deny "| count":name=tacacs:ready=le mock tacacs: listening on
expect=stderr:pattern=AUTHOR cmd="show bgp rib \| count \| json" reply=FAIL
expect=stderr:pattern=AUTHOR cmd="show bgp rib" reply=PASS_ADD
reject=stderr:pattern=AUTHOR cmd="show bgp rib count"

cmd=foreground:seq=2:exec=ze init:stdin=init-answers:exit=0
cmd=background:seq=3:exec=ze start aaa.conf:name=daemon:ready=Ze running.

# The command alone is allowed: the policy denies the chain, not the command.
cmd=foreground:seq=4:exec=ze cli -c "show bgp rib":exit=0
reject=stderr:contains=restricted by access control

# The chain is denied: the permission-denied answer, and no count.
cmd=foreground:seq=5:exec=ze cli -c "show bgp rib | count | json":exit=1
expect=stderr:contains=command restricted by access control
reject=stdout:contains=count

cmd=stop:seq=6:name=daemon:signal=term
cmd=stop:seq=7:name=tacacs:signal=term
